    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_3f7dfa1a",
      "@type": "OverallIntent",
      "instance_name": "overallintent_instance",
      "label": "the overallintent",
      "description": "meme-id: meme_image; hasOverallIntent: Harmful; overall-intent-description: The meme juxtaposes a human man relaxing in a kiddie pool with a gorilla in a similar pose, using the text 'after 3 million years of evolution we still love to relax' to imply a humorous or ironic parallel. While the intent appears to be lighthearted and satirical, it draws a direct visual and textual comparison between humans and gorillas, which can be interpreted as reinforcing harmful stereotypes by equating human leisure with primate behavior. This comparison may be seen as demeaning to humans by implying we are not much more evolved or sophisticated than primates, and it risks perpetuating the anti-Black stereotype of comparing Black people to primates, even if not explicitly targeting a specific race. The meme's overall intent is to create humor through absurd analogy, but the underlying comparison has the potential to cause harm by reinforcing outdated and offensive biological determinism and racial stereotypes.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
