    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_9324fc8b",
      "@type": "Scene",
      "instance_name": "relaxing_humans_and_gorillas",
      "label": "humans and gorillas relaxing in similar poses",
      "description": "The scene depicts a human man and a gorilla both sitting in a relaxed, cross-legged posture, visually juxtaposed to imply a humorous parallel in their behavior despite evolutionary differences. The man is in a backyard with a kiddie pool, while the gorilla is in a natural outdoor setting. The text overlay reinforces the comparison by stating 'after 3 million years of evolution we still love to relax'.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_da7759f1",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "anti_black_stereotype_primate_comparison",
      "label": "Anti-Black Stereotype: Comparing Black People to Primates",
      "description": "The meme's comparison of humans and gorillas, especially with the text 'after 3 million years of evolution', implicitly references a long-standing and deeply harmful racial stereotype that equates Black people with primates. This stereotype is used to demean and dehumanize Black individuals, suggesting they are less evolved, less intelligent, or more animalistic. The meme, by drawing this parallel, risks reinforcing this offensive trope, even if not explicitly targeting a specific race, by equating human leisure with primate behavior in a way that can be interpreted as demeaning to humans and perpetuating biological determinism.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_1e5724c0",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "evolutionary_determinism",
      "label": "Evolutionary Determinism",
      "description": "The text 'after 3 million years of evolution' implies a biological or evolutionary hierarchy, suggesting that despite millions of years of development, humans and gorillas are fundamentally similar in their leisure behavior. This concept, while scientifically inaccurate in its application to human behavior, is a common trope in memes and satire that uses evolutionary biology to make absurd or humorous comparisons. The meme relies on this concept to create its ironic tone, but it also risks reinforcing outdated and simplistic views of human evolution.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_9dff3f91",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "humor_through_absurd_analogy",
      "label": "Humor Through Absurd Analogy",
      "description": "The meme's humor stems from the absurdity of comparing a human man relaxing in a kiddie pool with a gorilla in a similar pose, suggesting that despite vast evolutionary differences, both species share a basic, unchanging love for relaxation. This type of humor relies on the audience understanding the inherent ridiculousness of the comparison, which is a common technique in satire and internet memes. The meme's effectiveness depends on the viewer recognizing the absurdity of equating human leisure with primate behavior.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_fabd0969",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "humorous_relaxation",
      "label": "humorous expression of relaxation",
      "description": "The man's relaxed posture, combined with the absurd context of fishing in a kiddie pool, and the gorilla's similarly relaxed pose, create a humorous and ironic emotional tone. The text 'after 3 million years of evolution we still love to relax' amplifies this humor by framing the behavior as both timeless and absurdly comparable across species.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_8c18e33a",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_1",
      "label": "Humans and gorillas are mapped as analogous beings who love to relax",
      "description": "The text 'after 3 million years of evolution we still love to relax' creates an analogical mapping between the human man (visual entity) and the gorilla (visual entity). The text implies that despite vast evolutionary differences, both species share a fundamental, unchanged behavior of relaxation. The visual juxtaposition of both entities in similar relaxed poses reinforces this mapping. This mapping is central to the meme's humor and its underlying harmful stereotype, as it equates human leisure with primate behavior, implicitly referencing the anti-Black stereotype of comparing Black people to primates.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_752f284c",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the human man relaxing in the kiddie pool",
      "description": "The user is projected onto the visual material of the human man (man) sitting in the grass with a toy fishing rod, as the meme's humor and comparison are centered on this human figure. The text 'after 3 million years of evolution' implies the user (as a human) is part of the evolutionary narrative being referenced, and the comparison to the gorilla is framed as a shared, universal human trait (relaxation) that the user can identify with. The user is implicitly 'me' or 'you' in the context of the meme's ironic statement.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_c3dd1072",
      "@type": "ToxicityAssessment",
      "instance_name": "toxicity_1",
      "label": "Harmful",
      "description": "The meme draws a direct visual and textual comparison between humans and gorillas, which can be interpreted as reinforcing harmful stereotypes by equating human leisure with primate behavior. This comparison may be seen as demeaning to humans by implying we are not much more evolved or sophisticated than primates, and it risks perpetuating the anti-Black stereotype of comparing Black people to primates, even if not explicitly targeting a specific race. The meme's overall intent is to create humor through absurd analogy, but the underlying comparison has the potential to cause harm by reinforcing outdated and offensive biological determinism and racial stereotypes.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_3e72016e",
      "@type": "TargetCommunity",
      "instance_name": "audience_id",
      "label": "general internet meme audience",
      "description": "The meme is designed for a broad online audience familiar with internet humor, satire, and visual memes. It relies on the audience recognizing the absurdity of the comparison between humans and gorillas, as well as the cultural reference to evolutionary determinism and the historical anti-Black stereotype of comparing Black people to primates. The humor is intended for viewers who understand and appreciate ironic or absurdist analogies, even if they may not be aware of the underlying harmful implications.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_ce986582"
      },
